{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Graph requires this callback even for a storage-only consumer. The\n delegation test must use only node_path; discovering edges would be\n an unwanted graph access, which this guard rejects. Traversal fixtures\n separately exercise edge discovery."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is on an explicit function with a body. It sits in fenced commentary, so it is a tracked justification.\n\n- **The reason is substantive.** It says `Graph` requires the callback, but this storage-only fixture must delegate through `node_path` alone. The function body is a deliberate `panic!` that rejects any edge discovery, so it never runs in a passing test. That is a real reason for zero execution, not just difficulty.\n- **It agrees with the current source.** `impl[StoredGraph/EvidenceStorage]` calls only `self.node_path(node)`. The test `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` goes through that delegation and never reaches edge discovery.\n- **The claim about other fixtures holds.** Other test files implement `Graph::outgoing_edges` with real behavior. These are `Memory` in `tests/agent.rs`, `Application` in `tests/contracts.rs` and `Memory` in `tests/validation.rs`. So \"traversal fixtures separately exercise edge discovery\" is accurate.\n\nThe citation meets the built-in's criteria. Whether it is accepted depends on the independent assessment the built-in requires, which is outside this review.",
